{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788422226738,"sessionID":"ses_f99bb14eaffeLRyeOsPWdJgTt0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_07b6e3a83aa247c9bacb8549","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/testbed/docs/dispatcher/finite_state_machine/index.rst"},"output":"<path>/testbed/docs/dispatcher/finite_state_machine/index.rst</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: .. _Finite State Machine:\n2: \n3: ====================\n4: Finite State Machine\n5: ====================\n6: \n7:     A finite-state machine (FSM) or finite-state automaton (FSA, plural: automata), finite automaton,\n8:     or simply a state machine, is a mathematical model of computation.\n9: \n10:     It is an abstract machine that can be in exactly one of a finite number of states at any given time.\n11:     The FSM can change from one state to another in response to some inputs;\n12:     the change from one state to another is called a transition.\n13: \n14:     An FSM is defined by a list of its states, its initial state,\n15:     and the inputs that trigger each transition.\n16: \n17:     .. raw:: html\n18: \n19:         <hr>\n20: \n21:     Source: `WikiPedia <wiki>`_\n22: \n23: Usage example\n24: =============\n25: \n26: Not all functionality of the bot can be implemented as single handler,\n27: for example you will need to collect some data from user in separated steps you will need to use FSM.\n28: \n29: \n30: .. image:: ../../_static/fsm_example.png\n31:     :alt: FSM Example\n32: \n33: Let's see how to do that step-by-step\n34: \n35: Step by step\n36: ------------\n37: \n38: Before handle any states you will need to specify what kind of states you want to handle\n39: \n40: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n41:     :pyobject: Form\n42: \n43: And then write handler for each state separately from the start of dialog\n44: \n45: Here is dialog can be started only via command :code:`/start`, so lets handle it and make transition user to state :code:`Form.name`\n46: \n47: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n48:     :pyobject: command_start\n49: \n50: After that you will need to save some data to the storage and make transition to next step.\n51: \n52: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n53:     :pyobject: process_name\n54: \n55: At the next steps user can make different answers, it can be `yes`, `no` or any other\n56: \n57: Handle :code:`yes` and soon we need to handle :code:`Form.language` state\n58: \n59: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n60:     :pyobject: process_like_write_bots\n61: \n62: Handle :code:`no`\n63: \n64: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n65:     :pyobject: process_dont_like_write_bots\n66: \n67: And handle any other answers\n68: \n69: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n70:     :pyobject: process_unknown_write_bots\n71: \n72: All possible cases of `like_bots` step was covered, let's implement finally step\n73: \n74: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n75:     :pyobject: process_language\n76: \n77: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n78:     :pyobject: show_summary\n79: \n80: And now you have covered all steps from the image, but you can make possibility to cancel conversation, lets do that via command or text\n81: \n82: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n83:     :pyobject: cancel_handler\n84: \n85: Complete example\n86: ----------------\n87: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n88:     :language: python\n89:     :linenos:\n90: \n91: \n92: Read more\n93: =========\n94: \n95: .. toctree::\n96: \n97:     storages\n98:     strategy\n99:     scene\n100: \n101: \n102: .. _wiki: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finite-state_machine\n\n(End of file - total 102 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":".. _Finite State Machine:\n\n====================\nFinite State Machine\n====================\n\n    A finite-state machine (FSM) or finite-state automaton (FSA, plural: automata), finite automaton,\n    or simply a state machine, is a mathematical model of computation.\n\n    It is an abstract machine that can be in exactly one of a finite number of states at any given time.\n    The FSM can change from one state to another in response to some inputs;\n    the change from one state to another is called a transition.\n\n    An FSM is defined by a list of its states, its initial state,\n    and the inputs that trigger each transition.\n\n    .. raw:: html\n\n        <hr>\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/testbed/docs/dispatcher/finite_state_machine/index.rst","text":".. _Finite State Machine:\n\n====================\nFinite State Machine\n====================\n\n    A finite-state machine (FSM) or finite-state automaton (FSA, plural: automata), finite automaton,\n    or simply a state machine, is a mathematical model of computation.\n\n    It is an abstract machine that can be in exactly one of a finite number of states at any given time.\n    The FSM can change from one state to another in response to some inputs;\n    the change from one state to another is called a transition.\n\n    An FSM is defined by a list of its states, its initial state,\n    and the inputs that trigger each transition.\n\n    .. raw:: html\n\n        <hr>\n\n    Source: `WikiPedia <wiki>`_\n\nUsage example\n=============\n\nNot all functionality of the bot can be implemented as single handler,\nfor example you will need to collect some data from user in separated steps you will need to use FSM.\n\n\n.. image:: ../../_static/fsm_example.png\n    :alt: FSM Example\n\nLet's see how to do that step-by-step\n\nStep by step\n------------\n\nBefore handle any states you will need to specify what kind of states you want to handle\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n    :pyobject: Form\n\nAnd then write handler for each state separately from the start of dialog\n\nHere is dialog can be started only via command :code:`/start`, so lets handle it and make transition user to state :code:`Form.name`\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n    :pyobject: command_start\n\nAfter that you will need to save some data to the storage and make transition to next step.\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n    :pyobject: process_name\n\nAt the next steps user can make different answers, it can be `yes`, `no` or any other\n\nHandle :code:`yes` and soon we need to handle :code:`Form.language` state\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n    :pyobject: process_like_write_bots\n\nHandle :code:`no`\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n    :pyobject: process_dont_like_write_bots\n\nAnd handle any other answers\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n    :pyobject: process_unknown_write_bots\n\nAll possible cases of `like_bots` step was covered, let's implement finally step\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n    :pyobject: process_language\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n    :pyobject: show_summary\n\nAnd now you have covered all steps from the image, but you can make possibility to cancel conversation, lets do that via command or text\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n    :pyobject: cancel_handler\n\nComplete example\n----------------\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/finite_state_machine.py\n    :language: python\n    :linenos:\n\n\nRead more\n=========\n\n.. toctree::\n\n    storages\n    strategy\n    scene\n\n\n.. _wiki: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finite-state_machine","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":102,"totalLines":102,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/dispatcher/finite_state_machine/index.rst","time":{"start":1788422226728,"end":1788422226736}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_06645830c001v2l9yOOetFJher","sessionID":"ses_f99bb14eaffeLRyeOsPWdJgTt0","messageID":"msg_06645810f001Jh4lfm35cwKPwx"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788422226785,"sessionID":"ses_f99bb14eaffeLRyeOsPWdJgTt0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_916b468ffd744ba98e0a5c2b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/testbed/docs/dispatcher/finite_state_machine/scene.rst"},"output":"<path>/testbed/docs/dispatcher/finite_state_machine/scene.rst</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: .. _Scenes:\n2: \n3: =============\n4: Scenes Wizard\n5: =============\n6: \n7: .. versionadded:: 3.2\n8: \n9: .. warning::\n10: \n11:     This feature is experimental and may be changed in future versions.\n12: \n13: **aiogram's** basics API is easy to use and powerful,\n14: allowing the implementation of simple interactions such as triggering a command or message\n15: for a response.\n16: However, certain tasks require a dialogue between the user and the bot.\n17: This is where Scenes come into play.\n18: \n19: Understanding Scenes\n20: ====================\n21: \n22: A Scene in **aiogram** is like an abstract, isolated namespace or room that a user can be\n23: ushered into via the code. When a user is within a Scene, most other global commands or\n24: message handlers are bypassed, unless they are specifically designed to function outside of the Scenes.\n25: This helps in creating an experience of focused interactions.\n26: Scenes provide a structure for more complex interactions,\n27: effectively isolating and managing contexts for different stages of the conversation.\n28: They allow you to control and manage the flow of the conversation in a more organized manner.\n29: \n30: Scene Lifecycle\n31: ---------------\n32: \n33: Each Scene can be \"entered\", \"left\" of \"exited\", allowing for clear transitions between different\n34: stages of the conversation.\n35: For instance, in a multi-step form filling interaction, each step could be a Scene -\n36: the bot guides the user from one Scene to the next as they provide the required information.\n37: \n38: Scene Listeners\n39: ---------------\n40: \n41: Scenes have their own hooks which are command or message listeners that only act while\n42: the user is within the Scene.\n43: These hooks react to user actions while the user is 'inside' the Scene,\n44: providing the responses or actions appropriate for that context.\n45: When the user is ushered from one Scene to another, the actions and responses change\n46: accordingly as the user is now interacting with the set of listeners inside the new Scene.\n47: These 'Scene-specific' hooks or listeners, detached from the global listening context,\n48: allow for more streamlined and organized bot-user interactions.\n49: \n50: \n51: Scene Interactions\n52: ------------------\n53: \n54: Each Scene is like a self-contained world, with interactions defined within the scope of that Scene.\n55: As such, only the handlers defined within the specific Scene will react to user's input during\n56: the lifecycle of that Scene.\n57: \n58: \n59: Scene Benefits\n60: --------------\n61: \n62: Scenes can help manage more complex interaction workflows and enable more interactive and dynamic\n63: dialogs between the user and the bot.\n64: This offers great flexibility in handling multi-step interactions or conversations with the users.\n65: \n66: How to use Scenes\n67: =================\n68: \n69: For example we have a quiz bot, which asks the user a series of questions and then displays the results.\n70: \n71: Lets start with the data models, in this example simple data models are used to represent\n72: the questions and answers, in real life you would probably use a database to store the data.\n73: \n74: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n75:     :language: python\n76:     :lines: 25-101\n77:     :caption: Questions list\n78: \n79: Then, we need to create a Scene class that will represent the quiz game scene:\n80: \n81: .. note::\n82: \n83:     Keyword argument passed into class definition describes the scene name - is the same as state of the scene.\n84: \n85: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n86:     :language: python\n87:     :pyobject: QuizScene\n88:     :emphasize-lines: 1\n89:     :lines: -7\n90:     :caption: Quiz Scene\n91: \n92: \n93: Also we need to define a handler that helps to start the quiz game:\n94: \n95: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n96:     :language: python\n97:     :caption: Start command handler\n98:     :lines: 260-262\n99: \n100: Once the scene is defined, we need to register it in the SceneRegistry:\n101: \n102: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n103:     :language: python\n104:     :pyobject: create_dispatcher\n105:     :caption: Registering the scene\n106: \n107: So, now we can implement the quiz game logic, each question is sent to the user one by one,\n108: and the user's answer is checked at the end of all questions.\n109: \n110: Now we need to write an entry point for the question handler:\n111: \n112: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n113:     :language: python\n114:     :caption: Question handler entry point\n115:     :pyobject: QuizScene.on_enter\n116: \n117: \n118: Once scene is entered, we should expect the user's answer, so we need to write a handler for it,\n119: this handler should expect the text message, save the answer and retake\n120: the question handler for the next question:\n121: \n122: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n123:     :language: python\n124:     :caption: Answer handler\n125:     :pyobject: QuizScene.answer\n126: \n127: When user answer with unknown message, we should expect the text message again:\n128: \n129: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n130:     :language: python\n131:     :caption: Unknown message handler\n132:     :pyobject: QuizScene.unknown_message\n133: \n134: When all questions are answered, we should show the results to the user, as you can see in the code below,\n135: we use `await self.wizard.exit()` to exit from the scene when questions list is over in the `QuizScene.on_enter` handler.\n136: \n137: Thats means that we need to write an exit handler to show the results to the user:\n138: \n139: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n140:     :language: python\n141:     :caption: Show results handler\n142:     :pyobject: QuizScene.on_exit\n143: \n144: Also we can implement a actions to exit from the quiz game or go back to the previous question:\n145: \n146: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n147:     :language: python\n148:     :caption: Exit handler\n149:     :pyobject: QuizScene.exit\n150: \n151: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n152:     :language: python\n153:     :caption: Back handler\n154:     :pyobject: QuizScene.back\n155: \n156: Now we can run the bot and test the quiz game:\n157: \n158: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n159:     :language: python\n160:     :caption: Run the bot\n161:     :lines: 291-\n162: \n163: Complete them all\n164: \n165: .. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n166:     :language: python\n167:     :caption: Quiz Example\n168: \n169: \n170: Components\n171: ==========\n172: \n173: - :class:`aiogram.fsm.scene.Scene` - represents a scene, contains handlers\n174: - :class:`aiogram.fsm.scene.SceneRegistry` - container for all scenes in the bot, used to register scenes and resolve them by name\n175: - :class:`aiogram.fsm.scene.ScenesManager` - manages scenes for each user, used to enter, leave and resolve current scene for user\n176: - :class:`aiogram.fsm.scene.SceneConfig` - scene configuration, used to configure scene\n177: - :class:`aiogram.fsm.scene.SceneWizard` - scene wizard, used to interact with user in scene from active scene handler\n178: - Markers - marker for scene handlers, used to mark scene handlers\n179: \n180: \n181: .. autoclass:: aiogram.fsm.scene.Scene\n182:     :members:\n183: \n184: .. autoclass:: aiogram.fsm.scene.SceneRegistry\n185:     :members:\n186: \n187: .. autoclass:: aiogram.fsm.scene.ScenesManager\n188:     :members:\n189: \n190: .. autoclass:: aiogram.fsm.scene.SceneConfig\n191:     :members:\n192: \n193: .. autoclass:: aiogram.fsm.scene.SceneWizard\n194:     :members:\n195: \n196: Markers\n197: -------\n198: \n199: Markers are similar to the Router event registering mechanism,\n200: but they are used to mark scene handlers in the Scene class.\n201: \n202: It can be imported from :code:`from aiogram.fsm.scene import on` and should be used as decorator.\n203: \n204: Allowed event types:\n205: \n206: - message\n207: - edited_message\n208: - channel_post\n209: - edited_channel_post\n210: - inline_query\n211: - chosen_inline_result\n212: - callback_query\n213: - shipping_query\n214: - pre_checkout_query\n215: - poll\n216: - poll_answer\n217: - my_chat_member\n218: - chat_member\n219: - chat_join_request\n220: \n221: Each event type can be filtered in the same way as in the Router.\n222: \n223: Also each event type can be marked as scene entry point, exit point or leave point.\n224: \n225: If you want to mark the scene can be entered from message or inline query,\n226: you should use :code:`on.message` or :code:`on.inline_query` marker:\n227: \n228: .. code-block:: python\n229: \n230:     class MyScene(Scene, name=\"my_scene\"):\n231:         @on.message.enter()\n232:         async def on_enter(self, message: types.Message):\n233:             pass\n234: \n235:         @on.callback_query.enter()\n236:         async def on_enter(self, callback_query: types.CallbackQuery):\n237:             pass\n238: \n239: \n240: Scene has only three points for transitions:\n241: \n242: - enter point - when user enters to the scene\n243: - leave point - when user leaves the scene and the enter another scene\n244: - exit point - when user exits from the scene\n\n(End of file - total 244 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":".. _Scenes:\n\n=============\nScenes Wizard\n=============\n\n.. versionadded:: 3.2\n\n.. warning::\n\n    This feature is experimental and may be changed in future versions.\n\n**aiogram's** basics API is easy to use and powerful,\nallowing the implementation of simple interactions such as triggering a command or message\nfor a response.\nHowever, certain tasks require a dialogue between the user and the bot.\nThis is where Scenes come into play.\n\nUnderstanding Scenes\n====================","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/testbed/docs/dispatcher/finite_state_machine/scene.rst","text":".. _Scenes:\n\n=============\nScenes Wizard\n=============\n\n.. versionadded:: 3.2\n\n.. warning::\n\n    This feature is experimental and may be changed in future versions.\n\n**aiogram's** basics API is easy to use and powerful,\nallowing the implementation of simple interactions such as triggering a command or message\nfor a response.\nHowever, certain tasks require a dialogue between the user and the bot.\nThis is where Scenes come into play.\n\nUnderstanding Scenes\n====================\n\nA Scene in **aiogram** is like an abstract, isolated namespace or room that a user can be\nushered into via the code. When a user is within a Scene, most other global commands or\nmessage handlers are bypassed, unless they are specifically designed to function outside of the Scenes.\nThis helps in creating an experience of focused interactions.\nScenes provide a structure for more complex interactions,\neffectively isolating and managing contexts for different stages of the conversation.\nThey allow you to control and manage the flow of the conversation in a more organized manner.\n\nScene Lifecycle\n---------------\n\nEach Scene can be \"entered\", \"left\" of \"exited\", allowing for clear transitions between different\nstages of the conversation.\nFor instance, in a multi-step form filling interaction, each step could be a Scene -\nthe bot guides the user from one Scene to the next as they provide the required information.\n\nScene Listeners\n---------------\n\nScenes have their own hooks which are command or message listeners that only act while\nthe user is within the Scene.\nThese hooks react to user actions while the user is 'inside' the Scene,\nproviding the responses or actions appropriate for that context.\nWhen the user is ushered from one Scene to another, the actions and responses change\naccordingly as the user is now interacting with the set of listeners inside the new Scene.\nThese 'Scene-specific' hooks or listeners, detached from the global listening context,\nallow for more streamlined and organized bot-user interactions.\n\n\nScene Interactions\n------------------\n\nEach Scene is like a self-contained world, with interactions defined within the scope of that Scene.\nAs such, only the handlers defined within the specific Scene will react to user's input during\nthe lifecycle of that Scene.\n\n\nScene Benefits\n--------------\n\nScenes can help manage more complex interaction workflows and enable more interactive and dynamic\ndialogs between the user and the bot.\nThis offers great flexibility in handling multi-step interactions or conversations with the users.\n\nHow to use Scenes\n=================\n\nFor example we have a quiz bot, which asks the user a series of questions and then displays the results.\n\nLets start with the data models, in this example simple data models are used to represent\nthe questions and answers, in real life you would probably use a database to store the data.\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n    :language: python\n    :lines: 25-101\n    :caption: Questions list\n\nThen, we need to create a Scene class that will represent the quiz game scene:\n\n.. note::\n\n    Keyword argument passed into class definition describes the scene name - is the same as state of the scene.\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n    :language: python\n    :pyobject: QuizScene\n    :emphasize-lines: 1\n    :lines: -7\n    :caption: Quiz Scene\n\n\nAlso we need to define a handler that helps to start the quiz game:\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n    :language: python\n    :caption: Start command handler\n    :lines: 260-262\n\nOnce the scene is defined, we need to register it in the SceneRegistry:\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n    :language: python\n    :pyobject: create_dispatcher\n    :caption: Registering the scene\n\nSo, now we can implement the quiz game logic, each question is sent to the user one by one,\nand the user's answer is checked at the end of all questions.\n\nNow we need to write an entry point for the question handler:\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n    :language: python\n    :caption: Question handler entry point\n    :pyobject: QuizScene.on_enter\n\n\nOnce scene is entered, we should expect the user's answer, so we need to write a handler for it,\nthis handler should expect the text message, save the answer and retake\nthe question handler for the next question:\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n    :language: python\n    :caption: Answer handler\n    :pyobject: QuizScene.answer\n\nWhen user answer with unknown message, we should expect the text message again:\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n    :language: python\n    :caption: Unknown message handler\n    :pyobject: QuizScene.unknown_message\n\nWhen all questions are answered, we should show the results to the user, as you can see in the code below,\nwe use `await self.wizard.exit()` to exit from the scene when questions list is over in the `QuizScene.on_enter` handler.\n\nThats means that we need to write an exit handler to show the results to the user:\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n    :language: python\n    :caption: Show results handler\n    :pyobject: QuizScene.on_exit\n\nAlso we can implement a actions to exit from the quiz game or go back to the previous question:\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n    :language: python\n    :caption: Exit handler\n    :pyobject: QuizScene.exit\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n    :language: python\n    :caption: Back handler\n    :pyobject: QuizScene.back\n\nNow we can run the bot and test the quiz game:\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n    :language: python\n    :caption: Run the bot\n    :lines: 291-\n\nComplete them all\n\n.. literalinclude:: ../../../examples/quiz_scene.py\n    :language: python\n    :caption: Quiz Example\n\n\nComponents\n==========\n\n- :class:`aiogram.fsm.scene.Scene` - represents a scene, contains handlers\n- :class:`aiogram.fsm.scene.SceneRegistry` - container for all scenes in the bot, used to register scenes and resolve them by name\n- :class:`aiogram.fsm.scene.ScenesManager` - manages scenes for each user, used to enter, leave and resolve current scene for user\n- :class:`aiogram.fsm.scene.SceneConfig` - scene configuration, used to configure scene\n- :class:`aiogram.fsm.scene.SceneWizard` - scene wizard, used to interact with user in scene from active scene handler\n- Markers - marker for scene handlers, used to mark scene handlers\n\n\n.. autoclass:: aiogram.fsm.scene.Scene\n    :members:\n\n.. autoclass:: aiogram.fsm.scene.SceneRegistry\n    :members:\n\n.. autoclass:: aiogram.fsm.scene.ScenesManager\n    :members:\n\n.. autoclass:: aiogram.fsm.scene.SceneConfig\n    :members:\n\n.. autoclass:: aiogram.fsm.scene.SceneWizard\n    :members:\n\nMarkers\n-------\n\nMarkers are similar to the Router event registering mechanism,\nbut they are used to mark scene handlers in the Scene class.\n\nIt can be imported from :code:`from aiogram.fsm.scene import on` and should be used as decorator.\n\nAllowed event types:\n\n- message\n- edited_message\n- channel_post\n- edited_channel_post\n- inline_query\n- chosen_inline_result\n- callback_query\n- shipping_query\n- pre_checkout_query\n- poll\n- poll_answer\n- my_chat_member\n- chat_member\n- chat_join_request\n\nEach event type can be filtered in the same way as in the Router.\n\nAlso each event type can be marked as scene entry point, exit point or leave point.\n\nIf you want to mark the scene can be entered from message or inline query,\nyou should use :code:`on.message` or :code:`on.inline_query` marker:\n\n.. code-block:: python\n\n    class MyScene(Scene, name=\"my_scene\"):\n        @on.message.enter()\n        async def on_enter(self, message: types.Message):\n            pass\n\n        @on.callback_query.enter()\n        async def on_enter(self, callback_query: types.CallbackQuery):\n            pass\n\n\nScene has only three points for transitions:\n\n- enter point - when user enters to the scene\n- leave point - when user leaves the scene and the enter another scene\n- exit point - when user exits from the scene","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":244,"totalLines":244,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/dispatcher/finite_state_machine/scene.rst","time":{"start":1788422226768,"end":1788422226782}},"id":"prt_06645833b001J0jJIjRBJW3GXf","sessionID":"ses_f99bb14eaffeLRyeOsPWdJgTt0","messageID":"msg_06645810f001Jh4lfm35cwKPwx"}}
